commentBody:
@SP

Because language changes.

(It's not the only source that supports the answer.  See Steven M.'s University of Rochester cite.)

@Steve L
Clearly, M-W starts with the idea that current usage accepts 'alumni' for 'someone, etc.' and therefore this is correct. So goodbye singular vs plural, goodbye masculine vs feminine. We're now all one undifferentiated wodge of graduand flesh.
You see why I hate this stuff, right?

@Steve L
Maybe they doesn't like that either; maybe they prefers 'child-in-law'.
All language nonbinary now!

@Isabeau

Did you see the last thing I asked you in the last puzzle's comments?

I asked you that if you disagreed with the cited authorities (who provided support that crocheting was a form of needlework), would you agree that if such support provided by experts in the field exists and is readily findable, then (despite your misgivings), the clue passes muster from a crossword editor's point of view?

@Isabeau

Sorry, I’m not trying to be argumentative. I basically agree with you the clue could have been a more accurate one. 

I was trying to make a different point, but I’ll let it drop. It’s not that important.

@Pat N
Don't forget Tina Fey is also an authoress. And Amy Poehler is a podcast hostess.

Maybe, just maybe, we don't need gendered versions of these words? English is not a gendered language. The pearl-clutching over this one Latin word is obnoxious.

@Katie

But in some cases, it is. Princess, duchess, countess, queen; actress, stewardess, lioness, tigress, sister, mother, aunt, wife, girl, woman. Cow, sow, ewe, all the good crossword animal names. We say she and her, but other languages don’t even differentiate the pronouns. 

But why sometimes and not other times?  And which is better?  Why is there a separate feminine word in Spanish if the masculine one ends in -o, but not when it ends in -e?

@Steven M.

They were probably trying to avoid the usual clue of a Taiwanese computer company, yadda yadda yadda. 

I'd probably clue it, if I were intent on the tennis use, as [Someone who usually doesn't permit returns] -- "permit" might be a little awkward, though, there...

@CB
Even at Oxford!:
<a href="https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/2025/03/20/university-of-oxford-latin-ceremony-gender-neutral" target="_blank">https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/2025/03/20/university-of-oxford-latin-ceremony-gender-neutral</a>/

@Katie

I guess you missed this sentence from my original comment:

"The vast majority of other sources disagree"

@Michael Weiland
Good point. As an alumna of an all-girls high school, I'll die on the alumnae hill, but yeah, it's a bit arbitrary. I think insisting on the correct Latin endings can be a flex, and a pretentious one at that. Is it contradictory to insist on alumnae while also forbidding actress? Word policing is a tricky business.

Interestingly in French, gender is quite entrenched. Occupations typically have gendered endings, like acteur and actrice but also musicien and musicienne.

@Deborah Gordon
I always thought having a special term for a female version of something was sexist. "Poetess" being a prime example. Why is she not just a poet? Is the idea of a lady poet so ridiculous that we need a separate word for it? Why do we need to specify the gender at all?

English is not a gendered language. Let's keep it that way.

