@lucky13
All true. But LaWP wasn't intrinsically about transformational grammar, so far as I can remember (although Lakoff's views and arguments were certain influenced by it.) But I read that book, oh, forty years ago . . 
FWIW, Robin and George divorced about the time LaWP was published. Did that have anything to do with it? Let's say "no." But they continued to teach together at UC Berkeley throughout their careers.

@The Sus

Steve L often inserts himself under the second (and more often the third) category in my disclaimer posted below: 

“I’m certain I’ll get told why these aren’t actually naticks and why someone else is superior for knowing these things instantaneously and that I can’t have any opinion or feelings about them other than total praise for the puzzle”

@Steve L

Ok call it Best Two actors. And if you are saying that two men will win more often than not, then you are saying there is still a disparity between acting roles/opportunities/respect for male actors. If that’s the case we ought to start addressing that more, and then keep the two categories until we do. (But it’s certainly the case for directing, and we don’t have separate awards for that?) But if that’s the case, then shouldn’t we keep addressing male actors as “actors” and female actors as “actresses”, since there seems to be some social distinction that might have relevance? Or is the hope that by addressing actresses as actors, that’s a step towards a solution?
It’s just a thorny issue with rabbit holes we don’t always investigate as throughly as we should.

@Caro -- GAUNT has always meant sickly looking and much too thin.  Usually there's some sort of bad health behind it.  "She's not completely over her bout of Covid, she's lost her appetite. and she's looking pale and gaunt."

Rawboned connotes big, lanky and healthy.  "He's a big, rawboned kid, just like his brother was at the same age.  I bet he has a future in Major League Baseball."

They seem like complete opposites to me.  I bet the meaning of GAUNT was changed when I wasn't looking.

@Andrzej I think anyone who has ever done writing or editing can attest that sometimes mistakes will get through.

I agree they know better, but eliminating mistakes in anything is impossible, and that isn't changed by being more knowledgeable or careful.

Again, I'm not talking about whether mistakes should or shouldn't be pointed out. I'm talking about how we respond when we notice those mistakes.

@Steve L

"When used in a more figurative sense (“a person who is a former member, employee, contributor, or inmate”) alumnus and alumni tend to be less gendered."

I missed this the first time around, and to me this is the most convincing argument for some wiggle room. If the point is that when used in the nonacademic sense, these terms are used more loosely, I might get behind that; because I can’t imagine an academic institution being anything but precise officially. For those who said this makes more sense because it’s SNL I can see your point now. I still feel like if I were an editor I would have avoided the controversy by using two males or a male and female; there was nothing to be gained here and it was still an error in judgment if not strict precision. This was not a misdirection or something to make the clue more fun or accessible, just an unnecessary provocation.

@SP

You missed it because it was on a different page than the one I first cited.

I agree with what you said about the non-academic use of the word.  You can subsitute "veteran" for the alum words in that context, but then they might actually still be on the show.  Of course, "former member" works fine.
